{"type":"system","subtype":"commands_changed","commands":[{"name":"imagegen","description":"Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":[".system__imagegen"]},{"name":"openai-docs","description":"Use when the user asks how to build with OpenAI products or APIs and needs up-to-date official documentation with citations, help choosing the latest model for a use case, or explicit GPT-5.4 upgrade and prompt-upgrade guidance; prioritize OpenAI docs MCP tools, use bundled references only as helper context, and restrict any fallback browsing to official OpenAI domains. (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":[".system__openai-docs"]},{"name":"plugin-creator","description":"Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, and baseline placeholders you can edit before publishing or testing. Use when Codex needs to create a new local plugin, add optional plugin structure, or generate or update repo-root `.agents/plugins/marketplace.json` entries for plugin ordering and availability metadata. (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":[".system__plugin-creator"]},{"name":"skill-creator","description":"Guide for creating effective skills. This skill should be used when users want to create a new skill (or update an existing skill) that extends Codex's capabilities with specialized knowledge, workflows, or tool integrations. (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":[".system__skill-creator"]},{"name":"skill-installer","description":"Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":[".system__skill-installer"]},{"name":"agent-engineering","description":"Universal agentic development patterns shared across framework-specific agents. TDD, ACI/tool design, error handling, observability, handoff patterns. Load on demand. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"agent-intel-plan","description":"Plan-before-query fleet analytics. Turn a plain-English question about the agent-intel warehouse into a structured analysis plan (numbered sub-questions, each mapped to a concrete read-only SQL query against the right central-v2 view, with the cost/model/billing gotchas surfaced up front and a report outline), block for explicit user approval on that plan, then execute each query by delegating to the agent-intel-query skill and synthesize one cited report. Use when an answer needs more than a single query, or when you want sign-off before spending query budget and context. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"agent-intel-query","description":"Run read-only SQL against the agent-intel analytics warehouse (the local central-v2 DuckDB file) by dispatching a subagent, then persist and index every turn's output to a per-project, cmux-workspace-scoped ledger that survives compaction and powers sequential-query recall — raw rows stay in the subagent, the primary gets a distilled summary. Use for fleet/cost/sprint/gate analytics any agent can run, with history. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"agent-migrate-to-brain","description":"Migrate agent from ./agents/ to brain/agents/, preserving Claude Code frontmatter (name, description, tools, model). Adds deprecation notices to skill references. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"agent-plan","description":"Plan new agents by analyzing role/purpose, selecting format (MD or JSON), mapping to skills, designing workflow, quality gates, and handoff patterns (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"agent-plan-rosetta","description":"Bootstrap specialist agents for unknown technologies. Deep-assimilates a repository, synthesizes a two-layer knowledge base (compact JSON fact graph + progressive-disclosure markdown docs), produces a Rosetta guide mapping familiar patterns to the new framework, and scaffolds a planner/implementer/reviewer agent triad so frontier models reason correctly instead of hallucinating. Trigger: /agent-plan-rosetta {tech} [{repo-url}] (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"agent-validator","description":"Discover and validate agent assignments against the live roster from brain/agents/ and ~/.claude/agents/. Detects agent type, extracts domain, suggests fixes. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ai-deals","description":"Research and discover free/cheap AI inference deals, promotions, and limited-time offers (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ai-roi","description":"Identify AI automation ROI opportunities for a business. Maps manual workflows, dispatches parallel research subagents, scores evidence T1-T5, builds ROI models with citations, runs adversarial review. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"apartment-find","description":"Find long-term apartment rentals and leases that allow dogs — searches Craigslist + Facebook Marketplace for 12-month+ rentals. Hardcoded dog-friendly requirement; auto-rejects listings that cap dogs at 1 (needs 2+ dog capacity). Pet-policy verdicts, lease-term parsing, scam detection, neighborhood safety tiers. Run /apartment-find to search. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ask","description":"Route questions to specialist agents and research tools without making file changes. Searches the local holocron knowledge base first for faster, contextual answers. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"assimilate","description":"Borg-themed assimilation engine — absorb a target's distinctiveness into the collective (holocron). Fans out crawler subagents that read EVERY file in the resolved target and return quote-anchored findings, gated by deterministic coverage and citation checks. Progressive-disclosure modes loaded on demand by RESOLVED TARGET KIND: repo (code repositories — default), analyst (institutional/market research), safe (repo supply-chain/security audit, graded A–F), patterns (published research reports — NOT code), creator (YouTube channel → crash course). Saves all output to holocron. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"bootstrap-computer","description":"Idempotent macOS setup: check current state, install only what's missing, using local configs as source of truth (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"brain-request","description":"File an improvement request or bug report about any brain element (skill, agent, doc, tool, script, hook, MCP server, config) as a GitHub issue on hackerpug-ai/brain, from any device, with no copy-paste. Use when a brain skill, agent, doc or tool misbehaves, lacks a capability, or needs a change, and a brain agent should implement it. Triggers: file a brain request, report a brain bug, request a brain skill or agent change, open an issue against brain, ask brain to change something. Wraps /change-request with brain's repo and labels. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"business-analyze-profitability","description":"Analyze software product ideas for profitability potential using market validation, profitability framework evaluation, and structured scoring. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"business-competitive-research","description":"Competitive analysis and market validation for product/business ideas using HBR frameworks (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"business-process","description":"Business process analysis using Lean, Six Sigma, and BPM methodologies (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"capture-media","description":"Ingest a meeting transcript and enrich your Heidi FDE notes repo from it. Extract a structured digest (decisions, commitments, asks/needs, requirement deltas), RESEARCH every relevant file/folder via the repo's own routing maps (AGENTS.md table + fde-os section 7 + people.md), then PLAN grounded, reconciled enrichments per file (NEW/UPDATE/CONFLICT/RESOLVE/SKIP) - contradictions become open-questions, never silent overwrites, and every addition cites the transcript. One approval gate, then apply edits and commit. Use when you have a meeting transcript (file path, pasted text, or Granola) and want to know which notes docs to update and exactly how. --dry-run plans only. External sources are read-only; never sends. The manual, plan-first realization of the fde-os capture skill (named /heidi-capture in the design). (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"career-network","description":"Search Justin's 1st-degree LinkedIn connections using any natural language query — returns ranked matches with personalized outreach openers (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"career-resume-build","description":"Generate ATS-optimized 1-page resumes tailored to job postings using ~/Projects/career data (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"change-request","description":"File a change request (a bug report or an improvement) as a GitHub issue on another project's repo you can access, so that project's own agents make the change instead of you editing their repo. Use when a skill, agent, tool, script, doc, or library owned by another project misbehaves, lacks a capability, or needs a change. Triggers: change request, file a change request, request a change in &lt;project&gt;, report a bug in &lt;repo&gt;, ask &lt;project&gt; to fix or change something, open an issue against another repo. For the brain repo, use /brain-request, which adds brain's labels. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"claudeception","description":"Continuous learning system that extracts reusable knowledge from work sessions. Triggers: /claudeception, \"save this as a skill\", \"what did we learn?\", or after non-obvious debugging discoveries. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nextjs-server-side-error-debugging","description":"Debug getServerSideProps and getStaticProps errors in Next.js. Use when:  (1) Page shows generic error but browser console is empty, (2) API routes  return 500 with no details, (3) Server-side code fails silently, (4) Error  only occurs on refresh not client navigation. Check terminal/server logs  instead of browser for actual error messages. (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["claudeception__examples__nextjs-server-side-error-debugging"]},{"name":"prisma-connection-pool-exhaustion","description":"Fix Prisma \"Too many connections\" and connection pool exhaustion errors in  serverless environments (Vercel, AWS Lambda, Netlify). Use when: (1) Error  \"P2024: Timed out fetching a new connection from the pool\", (2) PostgreSQL  \"too many connections for role\", (3) Database works locally but fails in  production serverless, (4) Intermittent database timeouts under load. (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["claudeception__examples__prisma-connection-pool-exhaustion"]},{"name":"typescript-circular-dependency","description":"Detect and resolve TypeScript/JavaScript circular import dependencies. Use when: (1) \"Cannot access 'X' before initialization\" at runtime, (2) Import returns  undefined unexpectedly, (3) \"ReferenceError: Cannot access X before initialization\", (4) Type errors that disappear when you change import order, (5) Jest/Vitest tests  fail with undefined imports that work in browser. (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["claudeception__examples__typescript-circular-dependency"]},{"name":"clean-code-plan","description":"Clean a codebase run after run, as Working Effectively with Legacy Code teaches: get code under test, then reshape it in small steps that never change behavior. Use when asked to clean up, refactor, tidy, reorganize folders, pay down tech debt, make code testable, remove duplication or dead code, or break up a long function or a large class. The first run in a repo reads the whole codebase and writes its organization plan and cleanup roadmap to docs/code-quality/; every later run starts from what changed since the last one, keeps that plan true, skips files other branches are editing, confirms scope, cleans, and logs. Scope: a change, a task, a sprint or mission, a PRD or campaign, or the repo, plus a free-text direction. Read-only finders return discrete fixes; you approve one plan; fixers work in worktrees; the project's own gates run before and after; a blind code-quality review joins. Agent-driven, no scripts. Not for bug fixes, features, or behavior changes: those are routed, not fixed. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"cmux-drive-process","description":"Drive an already-open cmux surface until a named skill finishes (planning, kb-run-sprint, or any other), then optionally spawn a new tab in the same workspace and send the next skill prompt. Use when you must watch a live cmux agent, nudge it when blocked, ensure every injected prompt is actually submitted (send-key enter), record the run by default, and must not edit product files or run the driven skill yourself. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"cmux-driver","description":"Persistent watch loop that drives orchestrator sessions across cmux projects. Observes via cmux socket API, triages events as AUTO/ESCALATE/INFORM, manages session lifecycle. Optionally augmented by ODIN Sentinel MCP for governance (readiness gates, structured closeout, watch cadence). (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"cmux-guard","description":"Persistent watch loop that enforces kb-run-sprint orchestrator discipline. Reads panes via capture-pane, judges V1-V9 violations with LLM reasoning, intervenes via send-key + Feed cards, caps + escalates. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"code-quality","description":"Review how well code is BUILT, separately from whether it does what was required. Use when asked to review code quality, maintainability, readability, reuse or DRY, composition of shared behaviour, testability, hidden side effects, units that do several jobs, library calls tangled into logic, leftover dead code, shared state or race conditions, or error handling; when a functional review has passed and the structure still needs a look; at sprint close, across everything that was integrated; or to audit an existing (brownfield) area and seed a known-problems list. Cross-language: universal questions that each carry an explicit hunt and an evidence rule, a lens per language (TypeScript, Rust), and a quality profile per repo. Agent-driven, no scripts. Returns a human-readable report: findings bucketed broken / sub-optimal / pre-existing, each with a fix and a next command; --json returns the machine verdict. Not for functional correctness, test quality, security, or performance. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"codex-agents-plan","description":"Analyze codebase context before creating or updating AGENTS.md. Spawns code-explorer (Haiku) for pattern discovery and code-architect (Sonnet) for deep analysis. Outputs project recommendations. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"comprehend","description":"Produce a structured comprehension report for any target — task, sprint, PRD, directory, codebase — using Nate Jones's 4-question Explanation as Artifact framework. Stores to holocron. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"contact-strategy","description":"Generate personalized contact strategies and non-spammy email drafts for people at target startups. Researches thought leadership, company context, and timing signals. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"craigslist","description":"Search Craigslist listings via web search. Supports location filtering, price range, category, and has-image checks. No API key needed. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"crew-gen","description":"Generate crew members from JSON templates with validation and storage in project crew/ directory (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"date-plan","description":"Plan romantic dates using research-first reverse interviewing. Researches local options in SLC/SF, asks targeted questions informed by actual available options, pre-checks reservation availability via Playwright, stores recommendations in holocron for tracking and preference accumulation. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"debug-red-hat","description":"Adversarial debugging that assumes bugs are in your code, not user error or external services. Examines code quality across 7 categories with confidence-scored findings. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"debugging","description":"Pattern recognition and historical analysis for debugging complex issues (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"deep-research","description":"Comprehensive deep research on any topic. Default is the on-device agent process (model-led decomposition, Jina/Exa/native ladder, quote-grounded findings, independence test, disconfirmation pass). Pass --mcp to use the holocron server pipeline (deep_research → deep_research_result) instead — durable sessions, code-enforced evidence gate, cancel/steer. Use for deep dives, local-file-anchored research, and iterative gap-filling. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"deep-research-swarm","description":"Fast parallel research via swarm of cheap workers. Manager decomposes query → 2-3 workers → synthesized report stored to holocron. Custom researchers via --agent. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"deep-research-teamwork","description":"Orchestrate a research team using Claude Code Agent Teams. 10-agent roster with tier-based sizing (T2-T4), iterative refinement, peer-to-peer SendMessage, shared TaskList coordination. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"dependency-research","description":"Research any dependency/library/framework patterns from holocron (first) then web (fallback). Returns patterns, implementation guidance, and best practices. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"design","description":"DEPRECATED — design-* agents retired 2026-07-25. Use the frontend-designer agent for all design tasks. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"design-prompt-json","description":"Generate design specification JSON files for all views, screens, and workflows in an epic's design folder (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"design-review","description":"Review a design reference against project implementation code, identify visual/behavioral/accessibility differences, produce a prioritized file-level adjustment plan. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"design-work","description":"Orchestrate Linear design tasks through pixel-perfect workflow with task tracking (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"devops","description":"CI/CD pipelines, containerization, infrastructure as code, and deployment patterns (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"dogfood-sc","description":"Plan and drive a Ship Commander dogfood as the Captain: from Claude Code or another harness with a shell and a browser, open a Commander chat in DSH Desktop and type into it. Writes a durable handoff and its owner-armed loop line (/loop in Claude Code, /goal elsewhere), runs a sprint as a new mission, audits stored sprint tasks against their source through kb-sprint-to-mission, and watches authorized work to its finish. Count-only imports cannot pass the report check. Diagnose breaks from native evidence, recover within the handoff's cap, and own routine diagnosis, recovery, fixes, deployment and rechecks within the authorized task. Ask only for a decision the owner must make. Use when asked to dogfood SC, load, run or complete a sprint and fix breaks, or exercise /sc-* behavior. Not from a Desktop chat on a Ship Commander project: that chat is the Commander under test and has no browser tool to drive itself. Use kb-sprint-to-mission for an import without the fix loop. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"e2e-testing-rules","description":"Comprehensive, harness-agnostic E2E test-suite PLANNING methodology — up-front journey map + UC×journey×tier coverage matrix + gap report, visible/holdout tiering, and the determinism seam for agentic products. Invoked by the PRD/sprint pipeline before tasks are created. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"explain-code","description":"Explain how a system actually works — or what a PR or diff actually changes — deeply enough to sign your name to it without reading the code. Traces the real causal path including the unhappy ones in a fresh-context subagent, runs safe probes so key claims are OBSERVED rather than merely read, and emits a layered Engineering Sign-off Brief: a plain-language answer, the mechanism, scenario cards, failure modes, rationale split into documented vs inferred, an anchored evidence appendix with a coverage statement, and a fail-closed readiness verdict. On a PR or diff it also ranks the key changes carrying the behavior (3 by default) while still inventorying every changed file. Emits self-contained HTML with diagrams when structure is the point. Use for 'how does X work?', 'what happens when Y?', 'what does this PR change?', or before signing off on unfamiliar code. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"feedback","description":"Get specialized agent feedback on project documents. Returns in-memory recommendations. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"find-target-companies","description":"Multi-mode AI company discovery — AI providers (infrastructure, models) and AI-first services. Prioritizes startups (10-200 employees, Seed–Series B) for higher impact and less bureaucracy. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"fix","description":"Automated bug fixing workflow with task-loop and Ralph Loop review. Use --explore for complex bugs requiring codebase understanding first. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"flights","description":"Find economical flights focusing on shoulder seasons (spring/fall). Shows price matrix calendar with smart recommendations and deals from travel blogs/sites. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"frontend-design","description":"Create distinctive, production-grade frontend interfaces with high design quality. Use this skill when the user asks to build web components, pages, or applications. Generates creative, polished code that avoids generic AI aesthetics. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"gdoc-sync","description":"Sync a folder of markdown files into a single Google Doc in My Drive (one-way overwrite). Pre-renders mermaid diagrams to images, rewrites relative .md links to GitHub URLs, forces a page break before each source file. Re-runs update the same Doc (URL stable, comments preserved). (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"golang","description":"Idiomatic Go patterns: context, error handling, concurrency, interfaces (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"graph-plan","description":"Build or improve a knowledge graph in JSON Graph Format (JGF v2.1) from documents, a folder, URLs, or the current conversation. The edge vocabulary is a versioned contract, drafted from competency questions and a survey of the sources, approved by a person, then frozen. You orchestrate read-only subagents and are the only writer: surveyors open-extract a sample, an architect drafts the contract, a challenger attacks it, extractors pull entity candidates and then type candidate pairs between resolved entities, and a blind judge audits sampled edges against their quoted evidence and answers the questions from the graph alone. Every write is validated with existing CLIs (check-jsonschema or python jsonschema, jq, grep) against docs/graph-rules. Use when asked to build, extract, extend, merge, clean up, or improve a knowledge graph, entity graph, or JGF file, or to turn documents or research into nodes and edges. Not for graph visualization or database query tuning. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"handoff","description":"Write a harness-neutral session handoff so ANY agent or human can resume this work without asking questions — Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Aider, OpenCode, a fresh session, a different model, or a person. Use when context is running low, before compaction, when switching model/harness/machine, when parking work overnight, at the end of a long debugging session, or when the user says handoff, hand off, pass this on, write a handoff, save state, or continue this elsewhere. Gathers ground truth by RUNNING read-only commands instead of recalling, labels every claim VERIFIED or CLAIMED or ASSUMED, and emits one self-contained Markdown file plus a copy-paste resume prompt. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"heidi-deep-research","description":"Heidi deep research workflow: fan out subagents across Heidi repos, Linear, Notion, Granola meeting notes, and local notes while preserving main-agent context; synthesize source-backed product and implementation answers. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"heidi-whats-new","description":"Heidi Slack + Linear what's-new briefing. Read-only swarm scans watch-list channels (#us-forward-deployment, #eng-integrations, #wt-revenue-cycle) and the Athena PBC + FDE Linear boards for movement, decisions, blockers, and issues to be aware of, then synthesizes a dated, acronym-glossed briefing into the notes repo. Use for a periodic 'what did I miss across Heidi FDE?' catch-up. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"holocron","description":"Direct query interface to the holocron knowledge database via MCP. Hybrid keyword + semantic search returns ranked results with relevance scores, excerpts, and related skill suggestions. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"inference-pick","description":"Match your use case to the best free/cheap AI inference provider. Scores providers by speed, cost, quality, free tier, multimodal, context length using holocron research + live pricing. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"init-project","description":"One-shot project bootstrap. Assumes local harness agents/subagents by default, while still wiring remote GitHub project controls: CI, CODEOWNERS, branch protection/rulesets, labels, and Issues readiness. Legacy reviewer-shim workflow setup is opt-in. Idempotent — safe to re-run. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"integration-validator","description":"Validate stub to real API migration during integration phase (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"interview-init","description":"Clone interview-template repo into company-specific directory and initialize project. Run init-project as part of setup. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ios-local-build","description":"Local iOS build pipeline — build IPA locally with EAS, upload to App Store Connect via eas submit, no EAS credits required (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"journal","description":"Use to create personal Claude Code learning journal entries with confirmation and indexing (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"jtbd","description":"Jobs to Be Done framework for product discovery and customer research (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kaizen-plan","description":"Plan a kaizen continuous-hardening test suite for ANY project around the two axes it manages at its core: the INVARIANT (what must always hold) and the STRESS DIMENSION (what you push to break it). CREATE collaborates with the human to define both axes, then derives the invariants×stress-dimensions catalog; REFINE re-evaluates the declared axes against the current code + business goals and tweaks them with the human (plus coverage/teeth/measurement/drift/ratchet/synthesize audits). Also specifies the determinism seam, tiered scenarios, the 3-layer findings ratchet, and scaffolds project-local operator skills. Grounded in chaos engineering, metamorphic testing, search-based/ramp testing, property-based shrinking, deterministic simulation testing, and mutation testing. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-e2e-retrofit","description":"Retrofit an in-flight PRD into Reality-Gate / e2e flow-coverage compliance. Audits a target PRD (e2e infra, core+edge flow registry, per-sprint compliance), treats every 'completed' sprint lacking passing gate evidence as UNVERIFIED, generates a CATCH-UP sprint that replays the shipped human flows for real (revealing what shipped broken) then fixes them, and re-plans remaining sprints to conform. Default dry-run; --apply writes the catch-up sprint + re-sequenced roadmap; never auto-runs the sprint. Triggers: catch-up sprint, e2e retrofit, migrate PRD to reality gate, remediate planned sprints, flow-coverage retrofit, fix in-flight project. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-enforce","description":"Watch all cmux/tmux Claude Code sessions and enforce kb-run-sprint orchestrator discipline using your own judgment. Spins up the kb-enforcer persona as a cheap always-on loop: observe each session, judge whether an actively-running orchestrator is breaking process (implementing or reviewing itself instead of dispatching specialists), and reach in via cmux/tmux to correct it — capped, then escalate. Stays out of any session the human is driving directly. Run in OpenCode on a free model or claude on a GLM base URL to keep cost low. Triggers: enforce orchestrators, watch sprints, kb-enforce, orchestrator police, compliance watchdog. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-improvement-plan","description":"Spec-driven entry point for non-PRD-shaped changes — ticketed bugs and general improvements without a tracker ticket. Creates an isolated branch+worktree, resolves specialist agents into INVESTIGATOR and CHALLENGER roles, then ends the investigation with ONE short decision report for someone who has never seen the code — what is wrong, why, each fix option, a recommendation, and where the work will land — cold-read by a fresh agent, saved to proposal.md, delivered as the turn's last message. A typed reply picks the scope, the target branch (default main) and whether a PR is required (default no). Posts the binding scope to the tracker in the first person, every claim carrying a link, quote, or judgment label. Once the code passes review and the scope audit, merges it into the local target; if a PR was asked for, opens it and answers its AUTOMATED reviewers: implements the right findings, pushes back with evidence on wrong ones, declines any that would weaken a test, leaves humans to you. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-improvement-tasks-plan","description":"Bridge a BINDING ScopeState (durable state file per brain/docs/SCOPESTATE-CONTRACT.md, --scope-json, or legacy SCOPE.md) into the canonical `.spec/tasks/{ticket-id}/{SPRINT.md + TASK-*.md}` shape that `/kb-run-sprint` consumes — with scenario-backed TCs that pass validate_scenario (a fakeability floor the validator enforces; no waiver flag exists). Optionally pre-dispatches `frontend-designer` to spec UI surfaces before the implementer runs. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-orchestrate","description":"Orchestrate a planned sprint OR a planning skill to verified-done across cmux surfaces. The leading parameter is the cycle (implement → review → plan); --implement picks a named implement skill (default kb-run-sprint; also kb-prd-plan / kb-sprint-plan / kb-sprint-tasks-plan). Spins up the implement skill, review-red-hat, the remediation skill, and kb-run-human-tests — each in its own cmux tab on a per-stage harness:model — arms each via that harness's goal, observes via the cmux socket API + durable artifacts, and routes implement → review → remediate → re-implement → qa until red-hat-green AND human-tests-pass (kb-run-sprint); planners run a 1-stage implement cycle to artifact-emitted (review is out-of-band). Owns the done-bit: a run loop never self-certifies. Also keeps a live world-model of EVERY surface and closes+removes a surface when done (300s drive). Triggers: orchestrate sprint, drive sprint to green, orchestrate a PRD/roadmap/task plan, implement-review-plan loop, drive every session to its goal. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-prd-estimate","description":"Estimate token cost by sprint and task for a PRD roadmap (or a single sprint folder): deterministic forecasts via the TOKEN-ESTIMATION framework (AC-count size classes covering planning, implementation, review, refactor fixes, and overage padding), measured actuals from real session stores, remaining-to-finish per sprint with live re-anchoring, and ready-to-paste --budget-tokens suggestions for kb-orchestrate. Run after /kb-sprint-plan (Fibonacci point stubs; legacy minute stubs still read), and again after /kb-sprint-tasks-plan for AC-based precision. Report-only by default; --stamp writes TOKEN-ESTIMATE lines into task files. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-prd-plan","description":"Plan and create PRDs as folder structure in .spec/prds/{prd-slug}/ by dispatching product, engineering and design specialists as unnamed agents per the Planner Handoff Contract (staged proposals, fail-closed on a lens that never delivered), full-feature scope, spec layer classification, semantic versioning. Technical requirements emit as a folder (one file per section) incl. a routing & views contract (route-vs-state discriminator, Route Delta). Optionally pins the base branch(es) this initiative's task worktrees land on — one record per repo, default land-on-trunk — so review-gated codebases land on a feature branch with a PR into main instead of on main itself. Optional Linear mode (--linear or a passed Linear doc URL) publishes/maintains the PRD as ONE remote Linear document under a Linear Project instead of the local folder. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-red-hat-loop","description":"Out-of-band adversarial review-and-fix loop for a planning artifact (PRD folder / ROADMAP.md / SPRINT+TASKs). Drives the artifact to zero blocking findings by iterating review -&gt; remediate (re-arm the authoring planner with the findings) -&gt; re-review, gated by the deterministic met() from PLANNING-REVIEW-GOAL-LOOP.md. This is the loop the kb-*/but-* planners used to run internally, now standalone and invokable on any artifact. Emits [goal:complete] / [goal:blocked]. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-run-human-tests","description":"Verify a sprint's human testing gate on one integration-ready candidate. A fresh independent judge drives every gate step on the real product. Exact same-candidate automated checkpoint receipts may be reused, but every human observation is fresh and identity-stamped before and after the step. Final pass requires all scheduled integration obligations current, the complete gate selection, one candidate/build/contract identity, and recomputed evidence. Diagnostic attempts can answer questions but cannot certify final acceptance. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-run-sprint","description":"Run a sprint's task files to reviewed commits landed on the trunk. One worktree per task, a specialist implementer that returns one run receipt per requirement, then a different specialist reviewer that reads those receipts before the diff, remediate until approved, the orchestrator merges and records the handoff the implementer's final report already carried, and the orchestrator may request planner-owned task updates after applying the five-question test, and withdraws a mis-scoped in-flight task when its implementer reports blocked or its cycles stop converging, so the sprint replans it instead of grinding. A question for the owner pauses only the work that depends on it; the run keeps moving. Once the sprint's work is verified, a behavior-preserving cleanup of the code it landed must finish before the human gate; no sprint is done without it. It rereads the plan's chain after each replan; a shared file no longer keeps ready tasks apart. Cross-harness (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Pi, Grok). (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-spike","description":"Time-boxed investigation that answers ONE engineering question by running real code before a directive is committed to. Takes a directive, grounds it in the real codebase or dependency, designs a falsifiable experiment with pre-registered machine-checkable predictions, dispatches subagents to build and run a throwaway harness against the REAL system under test (never a mock), computes the verdict deterministically from captured evidence rather than from any agent's narrative, runs an adversarial skeptic pass, and returns CONFIRMED / REFUTED / INCONCLUSIVE / BLOCKED with confidence, limitations, and a decision recommendation. Spike code is throwaway and never merged; spikes chain via follow-up directives to a bounded depth. Use before committing to an architecture, adopting a dependency, or whenever a plan rests on an assumption nobody has observed. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-sprint-experiment","description":"Prepare and run a multi-arm experiment on one kb sprint: same SPRINT.md as control (status quo) versus one or more treatments (skill, agents, or project overlay). Freezes the decision rule before any arm spends, isolates worktrees, invokes kb-run-sprint and review-red-hat per arm, harvests tokens, red-hat AC pass rate, review cycles, and stalls from real artifacts, and hands the writeup to /research-report under brain/docs/research/. Trigger on: A/B the sprint, compare skill change, experiment on kb-run-sprint, control vs treatment. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-sprint-plan","description":"Generate a sprint roadmap from a PRD. Each sprint is a VERTICAL SLICE around a HUMAN TESTING GATE whose steps COVER the sprint's tasks (coverage ledger: exercises/dark/demonstrates_ucs; no step caps) on a PRD-named product surface, RED at sprint start, ending with a FIRST CONTACT step: the person's own launch of the product on their machine as it is, quoting the UC's words. Dispatches the FULL planning-specialist SET (fail-closed) in parallel with UC-overlap fusion; the orchestrator consolidates — never hand-authors. Writes ROADMAP.md. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-sprint-roadmap-manage","description":"RETIRED 2026-06-03 — across-sprint concurrency is gone; concurrency is now intra-sprint only (see SPRINT-CONCURRENCY-DOCTRINE.md). Do NOT use this to orchestrate, fan out, or steer multiple concurrent sprint sessions. Kept for reference/salvage only. (Formerly: become the cmux-specialist and coordinate multiple concurrent cmux sprint sessions by roadmap dependency.) (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-sprint-tasks-plan","description":"Parse ROADMAP.md, generate SPRINT.md JIT, then expand to per-task files (TASK-TEMPLATE v5.5) with AC-N/TC-N criteria and REQUIREMENT-CONTRACT v1. Multi-repo aware — a sprint may span repos and every task is bound to exactly one. Enforces 115-point rubric. Run before /kb-run-sprint. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"kb-sprint-to-mission","description":"Load or import a kb-planned sprint into Ship Commander. Reads SPRINT.md and every task contract, preserves visible requirements and source versions, accounts for hidden checks, declares through Commander verbs, and compares every stored task with its source. Use for plain-language sprint onboarding, mission/task add, or a read-only fidelity audit of a draft or captured readback. A matching task count is not completion. Config and declaration confirmations use existing Captain delegation; an ask-first invocation waits for the owner. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"librarian","description":"Organize holocron after research. Validates metadata, deduplicates, ensures consistent naming, runs maintain.sh, reports health. Auto-triggered by stop hook on research/deep-research. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"linkedin-draft-post","description":"Transform prompts/outlines into viral LinkedIn posts using PAIPS formula, hooks, and engagement optimization (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"local-models","description":"Manage the local oMLX model library under ~/models/ — list and scan MLX weight dirs, download from HuggingFace, map fleet roles (reviewer/implementer/orchestrator/verifier), and register semantic model names into pi or opencode via the LiteLLM router on :4545. Triggers when the user says list local models, what models do I have, download an MLX model, register a local model, scan for models, which model is the implementer. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"local-serve","description":"Start, stop, status, and smoke-test the local oMLX coder fleet. Production entry point is the LiteLLM router on :4545 (model names: reviewer, implementer, orchestrator, verifier). Backend is multi-model oMLX on :8003 per Apple Silicon node. Triggers when the user says start the local LLM, fleet status, start omlx, stop local models, serve a local model, local router health, warm the fleet. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"mastra-patterns","description":"Mastra TypeScript AI agent framework patterns — Agents, Tools, Workflows, Processors (guardrails), Memory, Evals, Observability, Client SDK. Load on demand per concept. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"mcp-build","description":"Generate complete FastMCP server projects with best practices, templates, and installation scripts in minutes (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"naming","description":"Generate company/product names using research-backed framework with optional domain availability checking (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"native-sandbox","description":"Launch NativeSandbox UI component browser in iOS or Android projects. Handles simulator/emulator discovery, app installation, sandbox-mode activation. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"orchestrator-code-restriction","description":"Enforce separation of concerns — orchestrators must not change code during sprint/epic loops; escalate to planned agents for self-review and fixes. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pc-init","description":"Initialize a pi-commander sprint directory into state.json. Parses tasks, computes DAG, assigns waves, resolves agents, bootstraps config. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pi-agent-rust","description":"Speeds pi_agent_rust development and verification. Use when editing providers, tools, sessions, extensions, installer logic, or triaging regressions in this repo. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pixel-perfect-plan-plugin","description":"Plan pixel-perfect plugin updates with automatic version bumping and test tasks (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"plan-claude-plugin","description":"Base skill for planning Claude Code plugin updates with semantic versioning (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"planning-specialist-resolver","description":"Resolve the SET of planning specialists (all relevant domain planners + design) from a project's AGENTS.md specialist table (header-tolerant: Local Domain Experts | Specialist Agents | Domain Experts), infer from the live agent roster when the table is thin, and FAIL CLOSED (ask the user) when the set can't be resolved. Returns domain_specialists[] for multi-domain dispatch. Used by kb-sprint-plan and kb-sprint-tasks-plan. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"platform-config","description":"Define and standardize platform selections in AGENTS.md. Ensures mobile projects plan iOS and Android together. Standalone or integrated into /init-project. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"platform-detector","description":"Detect project platform configuration from AGENTS.md Platforms section. Returns enforcement mode (multi-platform/single/none) and selected platforms. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pr-create","description":"Use when you have a list of fork-local changes to send upstream as separate, unrelated pull requests — the fan-out sibling of pr-stack. Parses a commit-annotated changelog (or a commit range, branches, or prose), triages every change into upstreamable / fork-private / mixed / already-upstream / dependent, strips fork-private paths and re-scans the final diff for fork identity leaks, verifies independence instead of trusting it, builds each branch against upstream main before pushing, then opens one cross-repo PR per change and maintains them (status / sync / update / reconcile) until they merge. Pure git + gh. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pr-stack","description":"Use when a branch has grown too large for one PR, or when you have a backlog of integration-branch commits that need to be distributed to an existing published stack. Splits monoliths into dependency-ordered stacks (Phase A/B), maintains them as ancestors land (C/D/E/F), distributes late-arriving commits across tier branches via file→tier mapping with auto-split of cross-tier commits (G), and verifies ancestry + drift + cross-repo feasibility (H). Pure git + gh, no Graphite/ghstack/spr required. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"prd-reader","description":"Parse a PRD into structured content (sections, frontmatter, design references) from EITHER a local .spec/prd file/folder OR a remote Linear document (URL/slug/id). Used by kb-sprint-plan, kb-sprint-tasks-plan, and other PRD-consuming skills. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"prd-sync","description":"Sync PRDs between Linear (source of truth) and local cache. Pull from Linear or push legacy PRDs. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"print","description":"Convert markdown to PDF (default) or Word (.docx), export to ~/.holocron/exports/ (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"product-executive-summary","description":"Generate business-facing executive summaries for epics: MD + HTML, UX highlights, design callouts, demo-ready screens for stakeholders. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"product-naming","description":"Generate brandable product/feature names from a description with auto product-type detection, multi-TLD domain check, polarization check, launch considerations. Wraps /naming. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"prompt-optimizer","description":"Transform any input into precision prompts. ULTRATHINK protocol. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"react-validation-gates","description":"Universal React validation gates for production UI code quality and component safety (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"register-agent-bot","description":"Register and bootstrap the three run-sprint GitHub App bot identities for a repository. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"release-gh","description":"Create GitHub release with intelligent semantic versioning, commit message generation, and automated release notes (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"research","description":"Quick research. Default is an on-device single-pass web search (read top results, synthesize brief findings, store if possible). Pass --mcp to search the holocron knowledge base first and, when new research is needed, use the server-side quick_research pipeline (durable session, code-enforced evidence gate). Podcast URLs are detected and reported as unavailable rather than faked. Use for fast lookups; escalate to /deep-research for comprehensive multi-round analysis. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"research-loop","description":"Run parallel Ralph Loop research on multiple videos/posts (max 3 iterations each). Ideal for backfilling channel content or batch video analysis. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"research-product","description":"Answer product research questions (market sizing, pricing, positioning, competitive, validation) via a product-manager-led agent team using Jina/Exa, T1–T4 evidence tiers, and cited synthesis. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"research-report","description":"Write a self-contained research report after a measured run — an eval experiment, a spike, an investigation — so a later reader can verify the findings from committed artifacts or re-run them. Harvests provenance deterministically (SHAs, run ids, config digests, pooling keys) rather than trusting recollection, proves or disproves pre-registration from git history, forces a validity interrogation with cited evidence, and validates the draft fail-closed. Never re-runs an experiment, never edits one. Trigger on: write up the experiment, document the eval results, research report, publish the findings, record what we learned. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"research-to-practice","description":"Analyze completed AI-coding research and suggest improvements to skills, workflows, and processes. Auto-triggered after deep-research on AI/coding topics, or invoke manually. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"revenue-validate","description":"Analyze a codebase for revenue potential. Asks goal (venture, passive, break-even, charity), dispatches 7 agents with T1-T4 evidence governance, adversarial challenge, and DVF scoring. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"review-cli","description":"Run a TUI/CLI human-test gate (or a TUI scene script), capture a real terminal \"screen cap\" per test, flag failures with recommendations, and assemble a review-ui-style self-contained HTML report — while keeping structured JSON (findings.json / results.jsonl) as the source of truth. Use to make CLI/TUI gate runs scannable and visible, and to review terminal UIs (e.g. `spoke tui`) before the heavier VHS/golden-snapshot pipeline exists. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"review-product","description":"Fresh-eyes review of a running app that reports what is broken AND what would make it delightful. Use after a sprint lands, before a demo, or each polish cycle, when you want feedback on a feature set: what fails, what already delights, and what to change so it feels great. A spec-blind product-explorer subagent uses the app like a first-day user through whichever UI driver is live (Claude in Chrome, Playwright MCP, Metro, or cmux-cua), studies a screenshot of every screen, journals how each step felt, and files evidence-backed findings: dead controls, broken flows, data nonsense, confusing UX, design flaws, gore, moments that delight, and wishes (what it wanted, never how to build it), judged against a 'what great feels like' yardstick in its brief. A fresh sighted planner dispositions every problem; a fresh product-manager grades each job (broken, works, smooth, delightful) and ranks the changes that would make it delightful, each a requirement you accept or reject. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"review-red-hat","description":"Review a task, sprint, plan, or implementation with independent domain and test-quality reviewers. Validate consequential findings against evidence, preserve unresolved concerns, and produce a scoped recommended change set. Use for adversarial review before remediation or acceptance. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"review-remediate","description":"Stage 2 of UI review: take a /review-ui run dir and, directed by frontend-designer IN-SESSION and interactively, turn each flagged view's gap into specific delta-to-green tasks and EDIT the UI code in place to close them — asking the user at decisions, escalating major rewrites to collaborative planning, and verifying via re-review. Stateful (remediation.json), resumable (point at the run dir), with a LIVE report that updates as items are fixed. Approval = a re-review shows no HIGH/MED fidelity findings (fidelity &gt;=4). (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"review-ui","description":"Visual review and polish loop for UI surfaces. Survey mode: capture running app, review against mocks, cluster findings by blast radius, open interactive report. Polish mode (--polish): load findings from a prior run, fix one finding at a time with immediate re-capture and visual verification. Use to visually review built screens against mockups, track UI trends, and iteratively polish to production quality. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"rig-patterns","description":"Rig (rig.rs) Rust AI agent framework patterns — Tool traits, Extractors, Agents, Pipelines, VectorStores, ToolServers, PromptHooks. Load on demand per concept. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"rml-email-import","description":"Ingest emails from PST files into kai-process PostgreSQL database using kai-import CLI (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ro-doctor","description":"Diagnose and help fix rogueone run and environment problems. Runs the built-in dependency doctor, then investigates a run via its real session events.jsonl and DuckDB analytics (mc_events, mc_gate_outcomes, mc_sessions, mc_run_summary), pattern-matches failure signals to root causes — including park (`needs_replan` / unsatisfiable-contract operator queue) vs hang vs escalate — and proposes or — with confirmation — applies non-destructive fixes (resume, abandon/discard/relaunch, config repair, single-task re-run, conflict resolution). Treats the default-harness landing gap (ADR-0001) as a first-class differential. Agentic and flexible across many issue types. Use when a run is stuck, failing, parked, or behaving oddly. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ro-dogfood-loop-sprint","description":"Drive a rogueone LOOP run (`ro run --workflow loop`) to completion as a dogfood of rogueone itself: invoke /ro-loop-sprint inline, watch iteration-shaped progress, and treat every failure, spin, or deviation as product evidence — with loop-specific trigger classes (DRY-SPIN, STAGE-CONCENTRATION, CEILING-STORM, NO-CHECK, CACHE-REROLL, HOLD-ZOMBIE, TRUNK-CLOSE-RED, WORKTREE-DESTRUCTION, COEXISTENCE-DRIFT) feeding /ro-dogfood-sprint's intervention protocol [I-0]…[I-7] verbatim (analyze immediately, brief the operator, route by owning repo, fix only on approval via subagents, land on trunk or put a question in front of the operator — never silence). Adds the loop's own honesty rule: steering the run beyond answering typed questions is a counted intervention that scores against the PRD's zero-intervention acceptance. Use /ro-dogfood-sprint for full/baseline/holdout/redgreen; use /ro-loop-sprint when you just want the loop run driven. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ro-improvement-plan","description":"Spec-driven entry point for a single non-PRD change in a rogueone project — a ticketed bug or an untracked improvement. Establishes hard evidence, runs an adversarial fresh-eyes challenger, gates every scope option on LANDABILITY (a task with no machine-evaluable requirement lands nothing), renders every option's full proposal to chat AND to proposal.md before the human binds a scope — every option deliverable-first in plain language (what you get / what you don't get), machinery second — then lands that scope as REGISTRAR TASKS: emits a .spec charter, `ro sprint create`, delegates authoring to /ro-sprint-tasks-plan --scope-json, proves the push with `ro run --parse-only`, audits the authored contracts against files_in_scope, and hands off to /ro-run-sprint. Governed execution only — there is no ungoverned implementer path here. Use for a bug or improvement in a rogueone project; use /kb-improvement-plan for markdown-lane repos. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ro-init","description":"Initialize a project to use rogueone. Detects the tech stack, git identity, and installed harness binaries; INTERVIEWS implementer and reviewer for harness + model (ADR 0005 deleted roles.red); DISCOVERS specialists from AGENTS.md, wires distinct principals handles, and writes a project `.rogueone/agents.ts` pack whose knowledgeGraph paths are copied JSON files RogueOne inlines at dispatch (not path citations); writes agent-free reviewers[] and a valid rogueone.config.ts; validates fail-closed; wires env; runs doctor. Use when bootstrapping a repo, switching harness/model, or re-wiring review. There is no `rogueone init` command. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ro-loop-ab","description":"Run the check-land-loop PRD's acceptance comparison (UC-OBS-05): the SAME unmodified sprint on `full` then on `loop`, with a mandatory verified reset between arms (trunk back to the pre-run OID, task worktrees removed — fail closed on either check, else the second arm inherits first-arm lands and reports a success it did not earn), then a four-axis comparison from stored host events only (tasks landed, wall-clock, tokens, interventions via `ro metrics --workflow full|loop`) and a one-sentence verdict. Acceptance: the loop arm lands with ZERO operator interventions; `full` winning is a valid outcome. Prefers the productized A/B subcommand when `ro --help` shows one (OBS-005: ab-compare/run-ab); otherwise composes /ro-run-sprint (arm 1) + verified reset + /ro-loop-sprint (arm 2). The reset is destructive and always operator-approved, with arm-1 work preserve-tagged first. Not the eval-fixture experiment stack — for METRIC_REGISTRY questions with frozen scorers use /ro-run-sprint-experiment. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ro-loop-operations","description":"Reference for operating rogueone's loop run mode (ADR 0006, check-land-loop PRD): the check/land verbs (peek vs pop), `ro run --workflow loop`, the zero-branch driver contract (ready set from registrar depends_on, unconditional land on session exit, landed = git ancestry), `loop.*` config (required dispatchTimeoutMs fail-closed, worktreeTeardown, sessionPolicy, scratchFile notepad), shared `verify.timeoutMs` + `verify.exclusiveResources`, the host events (check_invoked, land_attempted, loop_iteration_started, loop_iteration), `ro metrics` operating questions, task-level hold via `ro steer answer`, the loop failure-signal map, and honest caveats (no park, no remediation router, no attempt caps, no liveness remediation; installed-release drift probe). Loaded on demand by ro-loop-sprint, ro-dogfood-loop-sprint, and ro-loop-ab. Workflow-agnostic protocols (working tree, no-dead-ends, session-resume sweep) stay in rogueone-operations §8/§9/§10. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ro-loop-sprint","description":"Launch, watch, and land ONE rogueone loop run in its OWN worktree, then ASK before merging to main. Creates a `sprint/&lt;id&gt;` worktree so the run never competes for `main` in the primary checkout; preflights the loop-verb/`--trunk-ref` probes, dispatch ceiling, registrar and landability; launches with `--trunk-ref sprint/&lt;id&gt;` so every task lands on the feature branch; watches iteration-shaped progress where a land DENIAL is normal respawn fuel; answers held tasks via `ro steer answer`; reports landed-in-run and integrated-to-main as two separate numbers. When the tasks are done it STOPS and asks whether to merge into main, with a dry run saying up front whether that would be clean or which files would conflict — silence is never consent. The merge is rehearsed in a throwaway worktree, so your checkout is never left conflicted. A dirty tree is COMMITTED by default with an agent-written message (`.rogueone/` excluded), not a question — `--on-dirty ask` restores the menu. Crash-resume = re-run the same command. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ro-plan-agent","description":"Plan, import, create, or update RogueOne project agents from a role description or existing Claude Code, OpenCode, Codex, or harness-neutral agent files. Use when a project needs local defineAgent/defineAgents profiles, a project-owned agent pack, exact source provenance, harness-aware tool/model decisions, and fail-closed validation against the installed RogueOne config. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ro-run-human-tests","description":"Verify a rogueone mission's human testing gate from the registrar and write each outcome back as an append-only evaluation. Reads mission_requirements rows (not SPRINT.md) via `ro mission show`, plans one real invocation per REQ-N seeded by the declared gate_entrypoint, checks the recorded gate_before no longer holds, drives the real human steps (real CLI/curl in a visible cmux pane; Playwright as a UI driver; Maestro for native iOS) through the borrowed kb-run-human-tests executor, recomputes every result deterministically, then calls `ro mission requirement evaluate --pass --evidence &lt;ref&gt;` or `--fail` per requirement — writing NO row for anything it could not run, so `pending` keeps meaning nobody looked. Optionally resolves a run suspended at gate_suspend via `ro resume --approve` / `--reject`, behind an ask gate that never approves on silence. Use for the ro/registrar lane; use /kb-run-human-tests for markdown sprints. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ro-sprint-plan","description":"Generate a sprint roadmap from a PRD, land it in rogueone's central registry, and render a read-only ROADMAP.md view of it into the PRD folder so a human can read the plan without querying the registry. Each sprint is a VERTICAL SLICE around a HUMAN TESTING GATE on a PRD-named product surface, RED at sprint start. Dispatches the FULL planning-specialist SET (fail-closed) in parallel and consolidates — never hand-authors. Sprints land as mission rows carrying gate_statement/gate_entrypoint/gate_before/proposed_by; gate steps as mission_requirements; PRD use cases as campaign_requirements; coverage is DERIVED by the registry, never self-reported — the fail-closed gate is `ro roadmap show --json` returning an EMPTY coverage.uncovered. The registry is the only STORE: the ROADMAP.md it writes is GENERATED by references/render-roadmap.py, carries `rendered_from: registry`, is drift-checked with --check, and is never an input despite the filename. Use before /ro-sprint-tasks-plan --sprint-id &lt;mission-key&gt;. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ro-sprint-tasks-plan","description":"Expand a sprint into rogueone REGISTRAR tasks — from a markdown ROADMAP.md, the internal ro roadmap (`ro roadmap show` / `--sprint-id`), or, with `--adhoc \"&lt;brief&gt;\"`, a sprint this run CREATES for work too big for one session and too small for a PRD. Dispatches domain specialist planners onto the v2 task contract (ADR 0005), scores the 115-point rubric, runs the deep seed-reality fakeability audit, then pushes each task's RICH authoring JSON through `ro task add --authoring`. Emits `version: \"2\"` with task-level `tests[]` and requirement `verification` refs — declarative, never a shell string. A PROVISIONAL mission (zero `mission_requirements` rows — beyond the commit horizon, or minted by --adhoc) is promoted by dispatching for its verbatim gate steps and appending them in ordinal order BEFORE any task; fidelity is DERIVED, never stored. Specialists author every gate statement and AC; the orchestrator only consolidates. Use before /ro-run-sprint. Not for kb markdown task trees — use kb-sprint-tasks-plan. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ro-upgrade","description":"Upgrade a project to the latest rogueone operator contract by reading docs/operator-changelog, detecting unapplied entries, and applying named migrations (e.g. checks_to_verifications_v1) with backup and Zod validation. Use after pulling a new rogueone, when config load fails with a migration message about config.checks, or when /ro-init warns the config shape is stale. Does not run sprints or author tasks. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"rogueone-operations","description":"Reference for operating rogueone — the deterministic governed sprint orchestrator. Covers immutable installed-runtime provenance and rollback; the workflow catalog (`baseline`, `full`, `holdout`, `redgreen`, `loop`); the v2 task contract (ADR 0005: tests[] + verification refs, universal rigor); the CLI surface (run/status/resume/stop/discard incl. the `workflow_run_control_unsupported` boundary); registrar CRUD and system-of-record rules; the `ro send` verdict channel; park→abandon→relaunch (`needs_replan`); session-resume reconciliation (§10, `resume-sweep.sh`); declarative verification; config; telemetry; DuckDB views; honest caveats; and (§11) the agent-hostile failure surface — registrar-id-only sprint tokens, the poison lease that deadlocks both `ro run` and `ro resume`, `ro stop` clearing it while reporting `ok:false`, minified Bun dumps whose actionable line is last, the 64KB `supervisor status` pipe hazard, and the `references/ro-launch-guard.sh` launch path. Loaded on demand by every ro-* skill. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"rosetta-ab-test","description":"Measure real-world impact of Rosetta KB injection by running the same test suite before/after and computing improvement delta (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"rosetta-bootstrap","description":"Create a new Rosetta KB: research (engine fanout) → write-graph.py --create → rosetta-sync. Trigger: /rosetta-bootstrap {tech} [{repo-url}] (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"rosetta-research","description":"Research an unknown technology for a Rosetta KB via the plugin's Mastra fanout engine ([A0]–[A6]). One path: detect harness, pass model, shell out. Fail closed — never a serial crawl. Trigger: /rosetta-research {tech} [{repo-url|docs-url}] (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"rosetta-stress-test","description":"Validate any Rosetta knowledge base by challenging an agent to implement a universal coding exercise using only the KB (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"rosetta-sync","description":"Sync Rosetta knowledge graphs into consuming agent bodies. Rebuilds the registry (exhaustive scan), validates registry↔filesystem, then HARD-OVERWRITES each agent's embedded knowledge block from the canonical graph. Trigger: /rosetta-sync [&lt;slug&gt;] [--check]. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"rosetta-upgrade","description":"Upgrade a Rosetta KB. Three modes. EXPAND (--topics): research a specialty at the current framework pin and merge it into the graph. REFRESH (--url, no --topics): research the framework's current version, diff, bump, re-inject. BENCHMARK (default): challenge before/after, then expand remaining gaps via --topics. All graph writes go through write-graph.py --update. Gate on Accept / Improve / Roll Back. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"rosetta-validate","description":"Adversarially stress-test any Rosetta knowledge base. Challenge an agent to implement a universal coding exercise using ONLY the KB, then run an adversarial reviewer against 28 universal anti-patterns to grade the fact graph and emit refactor recommendations. Trigger: /rosetta-validate {tech} [--challenge {id}] (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"run-manager","description":"Cockpit for the run-manager heartbeat daemon — a read-only status + conversation surface over your cmux project tabs. Answers \"what needs me?\", \"status\", \"rescan\", \"is the watcher up?\". The daemon (tools/run-manager/watch.ts) does the watching; this skill is how you interface with it from a harness session. Use when the user says \"run-manager\", \"manager status\", \"what needs my attention\", \"check my projects\", \"cockpit\". (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"run-sprint","description":"GitHub-PR-driven sprint orchestrator. Resolves implementer/reviewer subagents from GitHub Issue labels, dispatches them against task PRs targeting a sprint branch, governs them via Post-Turn Verification (deterministic check that they pushed / posted the review / left actionable feedback), enforces the 11-clause iron-gate, and orchestrator-only merges task PRs into sprint/{id}. Reviewer dispatch is temporarily pinned to the assigned local reviewer subagent, which receives the reviewer machine-user PAT as GH_TOKEN and posts the formal review plus REVIEWER-STATUS itself. Subagents own their pushes and reviews; orchestrator's primary job is GOVERNANCE. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"run-sprint-status","description":"Render a bounded /run-sprint status view from structured JSONL events and GitHub metadata. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"sc","description":"Ship Commander CLI orchestration. Run sc commands directly or use 'next' to pull the next ready task. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"seed-test-data","description":"Generate Convex E2E seed test data JSON from epic specifications (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"set-experts","description":"Multi-select domain expert agents and write them to AGENTS.md. Usable standalone or composed into /init-project. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"shop","description":"Fast parallel product shopping via holocron MCP across Amazon, eBay, Newegg, Best Buy, B&H, Back Market, Walmart. --consult for buying strategy, --research for category research. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"skill-audit","description":"Diagnostic meta-skill that audits skill ecosystem, maps dependencies, verifies documented behavior matches implementation, and safely tests samples (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"skill-format-logs","description":"Reformat skill logging output for terminal readability - important/overview info at bottom, details above (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"skill-meta-analysis","description":"Load and analyze multiple skill definitions without executing them - map processes, dependencies, and patterns (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"skill-migrate-skill-to-doc","description":"Migrate skill to brain/docs/{skill-name}/README.md, preserving artifacts in same directory (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"skill-migrate-to-brain-skill","description":"Migrate local skills from skills/ to brain/skills/, transforming Claude-specific headers (model, allowed-tools) to portable body content. Enables cross-platform skill sharing. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"skill-plan","description":"Plan standalone Agent Skills or docs: classify the artifact, design the entry card, and generate an implementation plan whose output passes a deterministic surface lint. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"skill-plan-brain","description":"Plan and execute coordinated changes across the brain ecosystem — agents (brain/agents/*.md), skills (brain/skills/), and codex-swarm JSON agents (brain/.AGENTS/*.json) in sync. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"skill-write","description":"Create new skills, docs, or update existing. Drafts from skill-plan's card-first skeleton, optimizes reference prose, lints the surface, previews, then writes on confirmation. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"skills-list","description":"Use when you want to see all available skills with descriptions. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"smol-epic-plan","description":"Decompose epic acceptance criteria into smol-coder-sized stories (1 describe block, ≤3 files, ≤200 lines) (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"smol-run","description":"JIT-plan and execute smol-coder tasks from .spec/smol-tasks/{epic-id}/ in dependency order (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"spec-review-swarm","description":"Parallel review of specs (PRD/TRD/design) against codebase. Auto-discovers reviewers from ~/.claude/agents/*-reviewer, dispatches tracks, synthesizes a unified report. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"spoke-debug","description":"Investigate a Spoke breakage: gather daemon logs + audit chain + gate history + run/step logs, correlate them around the failure, and CLASSIFY it as SPOKE-BUG vs WORKLOAD-FAILURE vs ENVIRONMENT — so the orchestrator knows whether to file a Spoke bug + fall back, or act on a real workload escalation. Read-only (never mutates state). Part of the spoke-* self-host suite — the diagnose leg of detect→diagnose→recover. Use when a run/gate/merge fails or the daemon is degraded. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"spoke-ingest-agents","description":"Ingest a project's target agents into Spoke as real signed principals (Ed25519 keypairs + scoped capabilities) via the spoke CLI. Two modes: documented (the project's AGENTS.md/specialists roster) or by-name (exact handles you pass). Idempotent — reconciles scopes with `set-scopes`, never retire+recreate. Use when onboarding a repo onto Spoke for agent-native sprint execution; part of the spoke-* self-host suite. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"spoke-ingest-plan","description":"Materialize a planned sprint into Spoke from kb-* artifacts: parse ROADMAP.md + SPRINT.md + task .md files (with REQUIREMENT-CONTRACT v1), translate the human-testing gate to a kb-sprint-gate-v1 file and each task body to kb-task-template-v5.2, then create the sprint + tasks (requirements embedded) via the spoke CLI. Idempotent, --json, fail-closed. Run after spoke-setup; part of the spoke-* self-host suite. Use when onboarding planned work onto Spoke. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"spoke-provision","description":"Provision a project's Spoke governance: migrate the universal base kb rules AND the project's per-directory/per-language validation checks into Spoke as verify-&lt;zone&gt; workflows, specialist routing, a merge policy, and branch protection. Detects language zones (platform-detector), proposes a governance manifest (ultraplan fan-out for multi-language repos), ratifies it with you (AskUser), then applies idempotently via --json. Run after spoke-setup + spoke-ingest-agents; part of the spoke-* self-host suite. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"spoke-run-sprint","description":"Run a sprint through Spoke: the kb-run-sprint orchestration brain (topo waves, per-AC requirement tracking, reviewer verification, iron-gate) with the substrate swapped to spoke verbs — implementer/reviewer dispatched under distinct signed identities in worktrees, PRs + signed reviews + CEL merge gate + merge all via the --json CLI. Includes a live event-stream monitor and a raw-git fallback when Spoke breaks. Run after setup + ingest-agents + ingest-plan + provision; part of the spoke-* self-host suite. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"spoke-setup","description":"Bootstrap a project onto Spoke: ensure the daemon (+ OS user-service), init a sandbox data dir, import or create the repo, install the kb preset, and wire the git remote + credential helper so `git push spoke` works. Idempotent, --json, fail-closed. The FIRST skill in the spoke-* self-host suite — run before ingest-agents / ingest-plan / provision. Use when onboarding a repo onto Spoke for agent-native sprint execution. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"strategize","description":"Multi-input Theorybase strategy analysis for ideas, codebases, orgs, or concepts. Evaluates sustainable business viability with framework-agnostic recognition + JSON output. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"stub-generator","description":"Generate Convex stub functions from API contracts for UI-first development (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"sublet-find","description":"Find sublets and rentals on Craigslist + Facebook Marketplace. Scam analysis, pet filtering, neighborhood safety tiers, headless FB via saved session. Run /sublet-find to search. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"subscribe","description":"Manage multi-source subscriptions via holocron MCP tools. All data stored in Convex cloud (cross-device access). (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"supabase-debug","description":"Supabase diagnostic — validates env, checks Edge Functions deployment, queries long_running_tasks and research_sessions via MCP, detects orphaned sessions and stuck tasks. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"superset-command","description":"Drive a running Electron app via Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) — click, type, navigate, evaluate JS, screenshot, and run multi-step action sequences over WebSocket. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"superset-merge-upstream","description":"Merge upstream into fork. Auto-resolves trivial conflicts, escalates feature conflicts to user via AskUser. Triggers: sync fork, merge upstream, pull origin, resolve merge conflicts. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"tdd-lib","description":"Shared TDD utilities for test execution, infrastructure checks, and verification gates. Used by kb-dispatch and kb-swarm. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"team-marketing","description":"Interactive marketing team orchestrator. Selects agents, confirms plan, spawns Agent Team, tracks via TaskList, supports steering and questions, reviews outputs, reports. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"team-product","description":"Interactive product team orchestrator. Discovers agents, confirms plan with user, spawns Agent Team, tracks via TaskList, supports steering, reviews outputs, shuts down and reports. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"test-plan","description":"Critical test planning with adversarial review. Runs test-review FIRST to find gaps, then collaborates with qa-engineer using TEST-PLAN-TEMPLATE for layer-appropriate Given-When-Then cases. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"test-skill-framework","description":"End-to-end automated testing framework for all skills with unit tests, fidelity detection, and regression tracking (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"toolbelt","description":"Dual-mode tool indexer/retriever. Save mode: fetches tool info from URL, saves to holocron. Query mode: hybrid search returns ranked results. Supports GitHub, npm, pypi, websites. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"tui-design","description":"Create distinctive, production-grade terminal UIs (TUIs) — Bubbletea, Ratatui, Textual, Rich, Ink, Blessed, ncurses. Use when building CLI dashboards, REPLs, file managers, log viewers, installer wizards. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"verify-feature","description":"Verify that functionality you name, describe, or point at (a task file or a Linear ticket) works in the real running app, driven end to end by agents with no scripts. Read-only recon subagents read the code for how to run the app, the screens and controls involved, and the flags, data, and sign-in it needs. The agent writes a plan with an expected value per step, shows it and asks before touching anything, starts the app, drives it with tools already on the machine (default: the website in Chromium via Claude in Chrome or Playwright CLI; Maestro, simctl, adb, or Metro for mobile; Playwright CLI over CDP for Electron; curl against WebDriver for an opt-in Tauri shell), records the screen, and writes a report with screenshots a person can confirm. Use when asked to test, verify, try, click through, or check that something works in the running app. Not for writing test files, running a suite, or changing product code. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"whats-new","description":"On-demand briefing on agentic engineering and AI dev tooling. Runs a Bun engine that fetches 39 sources in 7 classes, grades every finding, tags it NEW, UPDATED, or KNOWN against the last run, prints details first with the TL;DR last, then synthesizes a short impact analysis a human can read. Stores the report and analysis in holocron, or on disk if the store fails. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"brainstorm","description":"Brainstorm before any creative work (alias → superpowers:brainstorming) (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pixel-perfect:add-platform","description":"(pixel-perfect) Add a target platform to an existing project while preserving its existing platform configuration and progress.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pixel-perfect:assimilate","description":"(pixel-perfect) Analyze new mockups or inspiration and propose additions to the design system. Use evolve to reconcile refreshed designs with built components.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pixel-perfect:build","description":"(pixel-perfect) Implement or resume the confirmed component and screen plan. Use evolve when updated designs require reassessing completed work.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pixel-perfect:evolve","description":"(pixel-perfect) Reconcile an existing UI system with updated designs or requirements, plan visual and inventory changes, and coordinate implementation and verification.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pixel-perfect:init","description":"(pixel-perfect) Initialize a new UI project and record its goals, platforms, and toolchain. For an existing project, inspect status and resume its workflow.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pixel-perfect:refine","description":"(pixel-perfect) Apply a specific correction to named components, screens, or theme using feedback or a reference. Use evolve for broad reassessment or inventory changes.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pixel-perfect:research","description":"(pixel-perfect) Research UI patterns, products, or libraries and save findings for later design decisions.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pixel-perfect:scaffold","description":"(pixel-perfect) Set up the selected framework, tokens, component sandbox, and capture tooling before building product components.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pixel-perfect:status","description":"(pixel-perfect) Inspect current progress, reference freshness, unfinished refresh work, and the next appropriate command without changing project configuration.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pixel-perfect:verify","description":"(pixel-perfect) Check implementation, reference fidelity, and affected behavior; record evidence and incomplete work. Apply repairs only when requested with --fix.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"pixel-perfect:wireframe","description":"(pixel-perfect) Create low-fidelity layouts and state maps from requirements or concepts before implementation.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"ponytail:ponytail","description":"(ponytail) Forces the laziest solution that actually works, simplest, shortest, most minimal. Channels a senior dev who has seen everything: question whether the task needs to exist at all (YAGNI), reach for the standard library before custom code, native platform features before dependencies, one line before fifty. Supports intensity levels: lite, full (default), ultra. Use on ANY coding task: writing, adding, refactoring, fixing, reviewing, or designing code, and choosing libraries or dependencies. Also use whenever the user says \"ponytail\", \"be lazy\", \"lazy mode\", \"simplest solution\", \"minimal solution\", \"yagni\", \"do less\", or \"shortest path\", or complains about over-engineering, bloat, boilerplate, or unnecessary dependencies. Do NOT use for non-coding requests (general knowledge, prose, translation, summaries, recipes).","argumentHint":"[lite|full|ultra]","aliases":["ponytail"]},{"name":"ponytail:ponytail-audit","description":"(ponytail) Whole-repo audit for over-engineering. Like ponytail-review, but scans the entire codebase instead of a diff: a ranked list of what to delete, simplify, or replace with stdlib/native equivalents. Use when the user says \"audit this codebase\", \"audit for over-engineering\", \"what can I delete from this repo\", \"find bloat\", \"ponytail-audit\", or \"/ponytail-audit\". One-shot report, does not apply fixes.","argumentHint":"","aliases":["ponytail-audit"]},{"name":"ponytail:ponytail-debt","description":"(ponytail) Harvest every `ponytail:` comment in the codebase into a debt ledger, so the deliberate shortcuts and deferrals ponytail leaves behind get tracked instead of rotting into \"later means never\". Use when the user says \"ponytail debt\", \"/ponytail-debt\", \"what did ponytail defer\", \"list the shortcuts\", \"ponytail ledger\", or \"what did we mark to do later\". One-shot report, changes nothing.","argumentHint":"","aliases":["ponytail-debt"]},{"name":"ponytail:ponytail-gain","description":"(ponytail) Show ponytail's measured impact as a compact scoreboard: less code, less cost, more speed, from the benchmark medians. One-shot display, not a persistent mode, and not a per-repo number. Trigger: /ponytail-gain, \"ponytail gain\", \"what does ponytail save\", \"show ponytail impact\", \"ponytail scoreboard\".","argumentHint":"","aliases":["ponytail-gain"]},{"name":"ponytail:ponytail-help","description":"(ponytail) Quick-reference card for all ponytail modes, skills, and commands. One-shot display, not a persistent mode. Trigger: /ponytail-help, \"ponytail help\", \"what ponytail commands\", \"how do I use ponytail\".","argumentHint":"","aliases":["ponytail-help"]},{"name":"ponytail:ponytail-review","description":"(ponytail) Code review focused exclusively on over-engineering. Finds what to delete: reinvented standard library, unneeded dependencies, speculative abstractions, dead flexibility. One line per finding: location, what to cut, what replaces it. Use when the user says \"review for over-engineering\", \"what can we delete\", \"is this over-engineered\", \"simplify review\", or invokes /ponytail-review. Complements correctness-focused review, this one only hunts complexity.","argumentHint":"","aliases":["ponytail-review"]},{"name":"pixel-perfect:polish","description":"(pixel-perfect) Internal lens pack and findings schema used by design analysis and verification. Not a public command.","argumentHint":"","aliases":["polish"]},{"name":"pixel-perfect:process-context","description":"(pixel-perfect) Manifest-aware context for Pixel Perfect projects. Entry adapters load it explicitly to inspect phase awareness, manifest shape, and adapter conventions.","argumentHint":"","aliases":["process-context"]},{"name":"design-sync","description":"Push a React design system to claude.ai/design. This runs a converter that bundles the real component code (from Storybook or a bare package) and uploads it. Use when the user runs /design-sync or says \"sync my design system to Claude Design\".","argumentHint":"[<project hint, e.g. \"Acme DS\">]","builtin":true},{"name":"dataviz","description":"Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"update-config","description":"Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"verify","description":"Verify that a code change actually does what it's supposed to by exercising it end-to-end and observing behavior — drive the affected flow, not just tests or typecheck. Run before committing nontrivial changes; bootstraps this repo's project verify skill if none exists yet. Don't invoke it on a diff that only touches tests, docs, or other code with no runtime surface to drive (a change to product source always has one) — there's nothing to observe.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"debug","description":"Enable debug logging for this session and help diagnose issues","argumentHint":"[issue description]","builtin":true},{"name":"code-review","description":"Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs (plus reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups where the model's recipe covers them) at the effort level given first, before any target (low|medium|high|xhigh|max: from few high-confidence findings to many, some uncertain; ultra: deep multi-agent review in the cloud); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply them to the working tree after the review. Pass --max-findings <n> to report up to n findings, or --max-findings all for every finding. The choice stays until you pass --max-findings default. For ultra on a GitHub.com PR target, --post asks to post the finished review’s findings to the PR as a single comment from the user’s GitHub account (not a review; the launch dialog still confirms in interactive sessions, while non-interactive mode posts on the flag alone) and --no-post hides that option.","argumentHint":"[low|medium|high|xhigh|max|ultra] [--fix] [--comment] [<pr#>|<branch>|<path>] [--max-findings <n>|all]","aliases":["review"],"builtin":true},{"name":"simplify","description":"Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.","argumentHint":"[<target>]","builtin":true},{"name":"batch","description":"Research and plan a large-scale change, then execute it in parallel across 5–30 isolated worktree agents that each open a PR.","argumentHint":"<instruction>","builtin":true},{"name":"fewer-permission-prompts","description":"Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"doctor","description":"Health-check the user's Claude Code setup and fix issues: diagnose installation health — what the `claude doctor` terminal diagnostics cover — from local data (duplicate or leftover installs, PATH, unparseable settings files, broken or colliding agent definitions, skills whose frontmatter fails to parse); find unused skills, MCP servers, and plugins versus their context cost and disable dead weight; deduplicate local CLAUDE.md files against checked-in ones; trim checked-in CLAUDE.md files by cutting content a session could derive from the codebase (directory layouts, tech-stack lists, architecture overviews) while keeping gotchas, rationale, and non-standard conventions; migrate always-loaded CLAUDE.md guidance into lazy skills and nested CLAUDE.md files; flag slow hooks and context-heavy extensions; check the installed version is current; make auto mode the default permission mode; and pre-approve frequently denied read-only commands. Use when the user asks for a doctor run, checkup, audit, tune-up, or cleanup of their Claude Code setup or configuration.","argumentHint":"[prompt-audit [<path>]]","aliases":["checkup"],"builtin":true},{"name":"loop","description":"Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace.","argumentHint":"[interval] [prompt]","aliases":["proactive"],"builtin":true},{"name":"schedule","description":"Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule.","argumentHint":"","aliases":["routines"],"builtin":true},{"name":"claude-api","description":"Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"workflow-authoring","description":"Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"run","description":"Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"run-skill-generator","description":"Author or improve the run-<unit> skill - a per-project skill that tells agents how to build, launch, and drive this project's app. Use when the user asks to set up the project, get it running, write run instructions, or verify build/run steps work from a clean environment.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"plugin-authoring","description":"Make a mod: a change to Claude's own interface or behaviour, such as a panel or pane that stays beside the chat and updates, a band, status line, toast, slash command, or hook on tool calls. Use to make, change or debug a mod or its hooks module.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"advisor","description":"Let Claude consult a stronger model at key moments","argumentHint":"[fable|opus|sonnet|off]","builtin":true},{"name":"agents","description":"(removed) Ask Claude to create/manage subagents, or edit .claude/agents/","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"auto-mode-setup","description":"Teach auto mode about your environment, plus optional rule tweaks","argumentHint":"[--request-id <uuid>] (--wizard posture=… scope=… depth=… --propose | --expect-sha256 <64-hex> --apply-file <path>)","builtin":true},{"name":"autocompact","description":"Configure the auto-compact window size","argumentHint":"[auto|<tokens>]","builtin":true},{"name":"clear","description":"Start a new session with empty context; previous session stays on disk (resumable with /resume)","argumentHint":"[name]","aliases":["reset","new"],"builtin":true},{"name":"color","description":"Set the prompt bar color for this session","argumentHint":"[red|blue|green|yellow|purple|orange|pink|cyan|default]","builtin":true},{"name":"compact","description":"Free up context by summarizing the conversation so far","argumentHint":"<optional custom summarization instructions>","builtin":true},{"name":"config","description":"Set a setting by key","argumentHint":"key=value","aliases":["settings"],"builtin":true},{"name":"output-style","description":"List output styles or switch to one","argumentHint":"[style]","builtin":true},{"name":"context","description":"Show current context usage","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"effort","description":"Set effort level for model usage","argumentHint":"<low|medium|high|xhigh|max|auto|ultracode [on|off]>","builtin":true},{"name":"fast","description":"Toggle fast mode (Opus 5.5)","argumentHint":"[on|off]","builtin":true},{"name":"focus","description":"Toggle focus view: just your prompt, summary, and response","argumentHint":"[on|off]","builtin":true},{"name":"heapdump","description":"Dump the JS heap to the Desktop; on Linux with no Desktop folder, the home directory","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"init","description":"Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"mcp","description":"Manage MCP servers","argumentHint":"[reconnect|enable|disable [<server>|all]]","builtin":true},{"name":"import","description":"Import config from another AI coding agent","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"model","description":"Set the AI model for Claude Code","argumentHint":"<model>","builtin":true},{"name":"__remote-workflow","description":"Run the workflow script delivered in this session environment (server-launched sessions only)","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"workflow-launch-exec","description":"Execute a server-launched workflow handoff (workflow_launch event sessions only)","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"reload-plugins","description":"Activate pending plugin changes in the current session","argumentHint":"[--force]","builtin":true},{"name":"reload-skills","description":"Pick up skills added or changed on disk during this session","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"rename","description":"Rename the current conversation","argumentHint":"[name]","aliases":["name"],"builtin":true},{"name":"ultrareview","description":"Start a cloud agent that finds and verifies bugs in your branch (~5-15 min, $5-$25 USD) · Runs in a cloud session. See https://code.claude.com/docs/en/claude-code-on-the-web","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"security-review","description":"Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"usage-credits","description":"Configure usage credits or request them from your admin when you hit a limit","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"extra-usage","description":"Renamed to /usage-credits","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"usage","description":"Show session cost, plan usage, and what's contributing to your limits","argumentHint":"","aliases":["cost","stats"],"builtin":true},{"name":"insights","description":"Generate a report analyzing your Claude Code sessions","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"recap","description":"Generate a one-line session recap now","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"skill-doctor","description":"Show which loaded skills are unused and costing context","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"goal","description":"Set a goal — keep working until the condition is met","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"design-consent","description":"Grant Claude agent access to your Design projects","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"design-revoke","description":"Revoke Claude agent access to your Design projects","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"list-agents","description":"List subagents, teammates, and other Claude sessions you can message","argumentHint":"","aliases":["peers"],"builtin":true},{"name":"team-onboarding","description":"Help teammates ramp on Claude Code with a guide from your usage","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"built-in-browser","description":"Read this skill before the first step that uses the built-in browser, the browser pane inside the Claude desktop app (also called the in-app browser, the browser pane, Claude's browser, or \"your own browser\"), whose tools are named mcp__Claude_Browser__* when the session runs in the desktop app and mcp__remote-devices__Claude_Browser__* when a cloud session is linked to the person's computer; before those tools are turned on there may be a single enable__mcp__remote-devices__Claude_Browser tool instead. It covers the pane's persistent sign-ins, tabs and preview_start, reading pages as text, site approvals, what the pane cannot open, and what to do when it cannot be reached. It is not for Claude in Chrome (mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools), which has its own skill, and it does not decide which browser to use. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:built-in-browser"]},{"name":"chrome-browser","description":"Read this skill before the first step that uses Claude in Chrome, the browser extension whose tools are named mcp__claude-in-chrome__* (also called Chrome, the browser extension, or the external browser) and which acts in the person's real Chrome with their own sign-ins; before those tools are turned on there may be a single enable__mcp__claude-in-chrome tool instead. It covers loading the tools in one ToolSearch call, checking the person's open tabs and working in a new tab, site permissions, GIF recordings, console logs, dialogs to avoid, and when to stop and ask. It is not for the built-in browser (mcp__Claude_Browser__* or mcp__remote-devices__Claude_Browser__* tools), which has its own skill, and it does not decide which browser to use. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:chrome-browser"]},{"name":"computer-use","description":"Read this skill before the first step of any request to do something in an app on the person's own computer (Notes, Finder, System Settings, any desktop app), to look at their screen, or for \"computer use\". Computer use (desktop control) lets Claude take screenshots of the person's desktop and control it with clicks, typing and scrolling through the Claude desktop app; its tools are named mcp__computer-use__* when the session runs in the desktop app and mcp__remote-devices__computer_* when a cloud session is linked to the person's computer; before computer use is turned on for a conversation there may be no such tools, only an enable__mcp__remote-devices__computer tool, which turns it on. It covers turning it on, picking the right tool, the access flow, and the safety rules for tiered apps, links and financial actions. It is not for websites, which go through Claude in Chrome or the built-in browser and their own skills. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:computer-use"]},{"name":"anthropic-skills:deep-research","description":"Use this skill when the user's prompt requires (1) researching a topic across multiple sources, comparing options or alternatives, analyzing trends or history, understanding markets or industries, or reviewing literature or studies and (2) synthesizing that research into a comprehensive, narrative report. If you're planning to search the web or internal knowledge bases, consider using this skill. This skill coordinates research subagents, so use it only when you have a tool for spawning subagents (the Agent or Task tool); otherwise, research the question directly. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"docs","description":"docs (editable docs people share and comment on; the default for any document, named as a doc or not: a document, report, proposal, resume, cover letter, letter, contract, policy, form, template, worksheet, essay, handbook, guide, how-to, cheat sheet, SOP or other writing to keep, share, collaborate on, send, submit, print or sign; a doc exports to Word, PDF, Markdown or Google Docs, so needing a file to send, attach, upload, submit or print is no reason to pick Word, and a file nobody asked for is a doc, not Word; a plan, comparison, summary or notes asked in chat stays in chat; a pasted claude.ai artifact link may be a doc: check with docs tools first; Word or another file format named, tracked changes wanted, or a .docx to change or use as a template → that format's skill): making one → if no docs-connector instructions are in context, call its `guide` (topic.instructions) first; then create the doc (headings only, no body) before any search, file read or plan, even with files attached. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:docs"]},{"name":"docx","description":"Use this skill whenever the user wants to create, read, edit, or manipulate Word documents (.docx) or Word templates (.dotx). Triggers include: any mention of Microsoft Word Documents, such as 'Word doc', 'word document', '.docx', '.dotx', 'microsoft doc'. Also use when extracting or reorganizing content from .docx or .dotx files, inserting or replacing images in documents, find-and-replace in Word files, working with tracked changes or comments, or converting content into a polished Word document. If the user asks for a deliverable as a Word or .docx file (to download, email or print), use this skill. However, if they ask for a document, page, report, memo, or notes WITHOUT naming a file format and the session offers Claude's own dedicated document or page skill or connector, use that instead, even if they will email or print it. Do NOT use for PDFs, spreadsheets, Google Docs, or coding unrelated to document generation. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:docx"]},{"name":"google-workspace","description":"Read this before the first Google Drive, Docs, Sheets or Slides connector call whenever the task creates or changes a Google file. Use this skill whenever the user wants to create or change a Google Doc, Sheet or Slides file in their Google Drive. Triggers include: a request that names Google Docs, Sheets, Slides or Drive and asks to make, edit, format, copy or rename a file; a docs.google.com link with a request to change that file, even a one-line fix or suggested edits; and any follow-up change to a Google file from earlier in the chat, even \"change it\" or \"add a tab\". Includes helper scripts for document positions, cell ranges and slide layout. However, if the user asks for a doc, deck or spreadsheet without naming Google, or gives a Google file only as source material for something new, use Claude's own output type instead. Do NOT use for read-only questions about a Google file, or for Word, Excel, PowerPoint or PDF files. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:google-workspace"]},{"name":"import-memory","description":"Import a memory export from another AI assistant into Claude's memory — conversationally, additively, and with the content treated as data. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:import-memory"]},{"name":"morning","description":"Render the user's morning brief as a styled HTML artifact, or set it up as a recurring weekday task. Use only when the user explicitly asks to run, see, or set up their morning brief, or if they invoke /morning by name. A question about their day, schedule, or calendar is not by itself a request for the brief; answer it directly instead. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:morning"]},{"name":"pdf","description":"Use this skill whenever the user wants to do anything with PDF files. This includes reading or extracting text/tables from PDFs, combining or merging multiple PDFs into one, splitting PDFs apart, rotating pages, adding watermarks, creating new PDFs, filling PDF forms, encrypting/decrypting PDFs, extracting images, and OCR on scanned PDFs to make them searchable. If the user mentions a .pdf file or asks to produce one, use this skill. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:pdf"]},{"name":"pptx","description":"Use this skill any time a .pptx or .potx file is involved in any way — as input, output, or both. This includes: creating slide decks, pitch decks, or presentations as PowerPoint (.pptx) files; reading, parsing, or extracting text from any .pptx or .potx file (even if the extracted content will be used elsewhere, like in an email, summary, or creating a different type of slide deck); editing, modifying, or updating existing presentations; combining or splitting slide files; working with templates (.potx), layouts, speaker notes, or comments. Trigger whenever the user asks for a PowerPoint or .pptx file, or references a .pptx or .potx filename, regardless of what they plan to do with the content afterward. However, when the user asks for a deck, slides, a slide deck, or a presentation without naming a file format, default to using a dedicated slide-deck artifact type or a separate slides skill if this session offers one; otherwise, use this skill. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:pptx"]},{"name":"anthropic-skills:skill-creator","description":"Create new skills, modify and improve existing skills, and measure skill performance. Use when users want to create a skill from scratch, edit, or optimize an existing skill, run evals to test a skill, benchmark skill performance with variance analysis, or optimize a skill's description for better triggering accuracy. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"xlsx","description":"Use this skill any time a spreadsheet file is the primary input or output. This means any task where the user wants to: open, read, edit, or fix an existing .xlsx, .xlsm, .xltx, .csv, or .tsv file (e.g., adding columns, computing formulas, formatting, charting, cleaning messy data); create a new spreadsheet from scratch or from other data sources; or convert between tabular file formats. Trigger especially when the user references a spreadsheet file by name or path — even casually (like \"the xlsx in my downloads\") — and wants something done to it or produced from it. Also trigger for cleaning or restructuring messy tabular data files (malformed rows, misplaced headers, junk data) into proper spreadsheets. The deliverable must be a spreadsheet file. Do NOT trigger when the primary deliverable is a Word document, HTML report, standalone Python script, database pipeline, or Google Sheets API integration, even if tabular data is involved. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:xlsx"]},{"name":"exa:web_search_help (MCP)","description":"Get help with web search using Exa","argumentHint":""},{"name":"hubspot-rml:analyze-contacts-lifecycle-stages (MCP)","description":"Tracks HubSpot contacts created last quarter through each lifecycle stage.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"hubspot-rml:analyze-customer-tickets (MCP)","description":"Analyzes HubSpot tickets for this year to identify the top 10 problems your customers face.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"hubspot-rml:analyze-successful-customer-journeys (MCP)","description":"Shows the complete progression from first website visit to closed-won status for won deals in HubSpot in the last 3 months.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"hubspot-rml:list-my-assigned-contacts (MCP)","description":"Shows HubSpot contacts assigned to you this year.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"hubspot-rml:list-my-assigned-tickets (MCP)","description":"Shows all open HubSpot tickets assigned to you for this month.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"hubspot-rml:check-connector-status (MCP)","description":"Reviews HubSpot connector access details and permissions, checking if reauthorization is needed.","argumentHint":""},{"name":"metro:debug-app (MCP)","description":"Start a general debugging session: connect, check logs, inspect state","argumentHint":""},{"name":"metro:debug-errors (MCP)","description":"Investigate errors: get stack traces, symbolicate, find root cause","argumentHint":""},{"name":"metro:debug-performance (MCP)","description":"Profile JS CPU usage and React render performance, then summarize findings with a flamegraph","argumentHint":""},{"name":"metro:diagnose-network (MCP)","description":"Diagnose network issues: inspect requests and failures","argumentHint":""},{"name":"metro:trace-action (MCP)","description":"Trace a user action through Redux state changes and network calls","argumentHint":""},{"name":"metro:record-test (MCP)","description":"Record and generate an automated test (Appium, Maestro, or Detox) by navigating a user flow in the app","argumentHint":""},{"name":"metro:write-e2e-test (MCP)","description":"Write a complete E2E test by recording a flow with full setup: handles format-specific config, uses wait tools for reliability, and saves the recording","argumentHint":""},{"name":"metro:investigate-memory-leak (MCP)","description":"Systematically investigate a memory leak: capture baseline, reproduce the leak, compare heap allocations","argumentHint":""},{"name":"metro:debug-crash (MCP)","description":"Systematically investigate a crash or fatal error: collect errors, symbolicate stack traces, capture context","argumentHint":""},{"name":"metro:audit-accessibility (MCP)","description":"Run an accessibility audit and provide fixes","argumentHint":""}],"uuid":"12106da6-ba4a-4918-a8fa-a3b3ce17f1e3","session_id":"45648462-b0a4-4068-89a3-d816d9a7e264"}
